 ## Overview
-Voxel-based Ray Tracing is a global illumination (GI) and specular reflection fallback method that performs Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) traversal through a sparse, block-occupancy voxel grid to sample indirect lighting and environment reflections [S1, unofficial]. It serves as a drop-in replacement for Screen-Space Ray Tracing (SSRT) for GI and a fallback for Screen-Space Reflections (SSR) when screen-space coverage is incomplete, combining sky occlusion with Light Propagation Volumes (LPV) colour bleeding [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard voxel raycasting which often relies on volume density, this method operates on a binary block-occupancy grid to determine surface hits for lighting queries [S1, unofficial].
+Voxel-based Ray Tracing is a global illumination (GI) and specular reflection fallback method that performs Digital Differential Analyzer (DDA) traversal through a $128^3$ block-occupancy voxel grid to sample indirect lighting and environment reflections [S1, unofficial]. It serves as a drop-in replacement for Screen-Space Ray Tracing (SSRT) for GI and a fallback for Screen-Space Reflections (SSR) when screen-space coverage is incomplete, combining sky occlusion with Light Propagation Volumes (LPV) colour bleeding [S1, unofficial]. Unlike standard voxel raycasting which often relies on volume density, this method operates on a binary block-occupancy grid to determine surface hits for lighting queries [S1, unofficial].
 
